Ji Deren et al. v. Yancheng Municipal People's Government — administrative decision dispute

Plaintiffs: Ji Deren, male, 36 years old, individual transport operator, residing in Nanyang Town, Yancheng City, Jiangsu Province.

Plaintiffs: Cai Yuehua, male, 24 years old, individual transport operator, residing in Wuyou Town, Yancheng City, Jiangsu Province.

Plaintiffs: Cai Heping, male, 28 years old, individual transport operator, residing in Nanyang Town, Yancheng City, Jiangsu Province.

Plaintiffs: Ding Shuquan, male, 29 years old, individual transport operator, residing in Nanyang Town, Yancheng City, Jiangsu Province.

Defendant: Yancheng Municipal People's Government, Jiangsu Province. Domicile: Jianjun Middle Road, Yancheng City, Jiangsu Province.

Legal Representative: Tao Peirong, Mayor of the Municipality.

On August 20, 2002, the Yancheng Municipal People's Government issued the No. 13 Special Topic Meeting Minutes (hereinafter referred to as the "Meeting Minutes"), the main contents of which included: the scope of urban public transport shall be defined within the approved urban planning area, with the urban planning area as the boundary, and the construction and transportation departments shall each take their own responsibilities and perform their respective duties; the existing main lines opened by urban public transport within the planning area shall ensure normal operation and continue to be exempted from relevant transportation charges; disputes arising in urban public passenger transport within the planning area shall be coordinated by the government, and no rash actions shall be allowed, otherwise the responsible parties shall be held accountable. Ji Deren, Cai Yuehua, Cai Heping, and Ding Shuquan considered the Meeting Minutes an illegal administrative decision and filed a lawsuit with the Yancheng Intermediate People's Court, Jiangsu Province.

Plaintiffs' Allegations: The plaintiffs, Ji Deren, Cai Yuehua, Cai Heping, and Ding Shuquan, alleged that Routes 5 and 15 of the Yancheng Public Transport General Company (hereinafter referred to as the "Public Transport Company") extended beyond the urban area of Yancheng City without approval, overlapping with the plaintiffs' approved passenger transport routes, constituting unfair competition and harming their business interests. For this reason, they repeatedly reported to the Yancheng City Urban District Transportation Bureau (hereinafter referred to as the "Urban District Transportation Bureau"), requesting the bureau to lawfully penalize the Public Transport Company and its vehicles operating on Routes 5 and 15 involved in passenger transport and to recover illegal gains. The Meeting Minutes of the Yancheng Municipal People's Government interfered with the Urban District Transportation Bureau's investigation and punishment, violated relevant legal provisions, and directly damaged the plaintiffs' economic interests.

The plaintiffs' claims were: (1) To confirm that the defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, unlawfully forced the Urban District Transportation Bureau to stop its investigation and punishment of the Public Transport Company's illegal acts. (2) To confirm that the relevant contents of the Meeting Minutes violated the relevant provisions of the state's road transport management.

The main evidence submitted by the plaintiffs included:

1. Road transport permits and other relevant materials issued by the transportation management department to Ji Deren et al., as well as evidence of Ji Deren et al. paying relevant transportation charges;
2. Written materials submitted by Ji Deren et al. to the Urban District Transportation Bureau requesting it to lawfully perform its statutory duties;
3. Notices, letters, and replies from the Urban District Transportation Bureau to the Public Transport Company;
4. The reply of the Urban District Transportation Bureau, Urban Transport [2002] No. 66, "Reply to the Application of Operators of Passenger Transport Business on Provincial Highway 331 Requesting Our Bureau to Lawfully Perform its Statutory Duties of Managing the Transport Market."

The laws and regulatory documents provided by the plaintiffs included:

1. The 1998 Fixed Functions, Institutions, and Staffing Plan of the Ministry of Construction;
2. The 1998 Fixed Functions, Institutions, and Staffing Plan of the Ministry of Communications;
3. The 2000 Fixed Functions, Institutions, and Staffing Plan of the Jiangsu Provincial Department of Construction;
4. The 2000 Fixed Functions, Institutions, and Staffing Plan of the Jiangsu Provincial Department of Communications;
5. Reply of the General Office of the Ministry of Communications, Highway Document [1999] No. 22, "Reply on Clarifying the Main Body for Managing the Road Passenger Transport Industry";
6. Reply of the Ministry of Communications, Communications Letter Highway [1999] No. 189, "Reply to the Urgent Request for Clarification on Clarifying the Main Body for Managing the Road Passenger Transport Industry in Our City";
7. Letter of the Highway Department of the Ministry of Communications, Public Transport Management Document [2000] No. 216, "Letter Requesting the Prompt Rationalization of the Road Passenger Transport Management System and Related Matters in Xihua County";
8. 2000 Reply of the General Office of the Ministry of Communications, "Reply on the Main Body for Managing the Road Passenger Transport Industry";
9. Notice of the Jiangsu Provincial Department of Communications, Su Transportation [1999] No. 49, "Notice on Strengthening Transportation Industry Management of Urban Public Transport Enterprises Operating Road Passenger Transport";
10. Measures of Jiangsu Province for Implementing the Urban Planning Law of the People's Republic of China;
11. Notice of the State Development Planning Commission, Ministry of Finance, and Ministry of Communications, Price Management [1998] No. 1104, "Notice on Regulating Highway Passenger and Freight Surcharges to Increase Highway Construction Funds," dated June 17, 1998;
12. Joint Notice of the Ministry of Communications, State Planning Commission, Ministry of Finance, and State Price Bureau, (91) Communication Work No. 714, "Notice on Issuing the 'Regulations on the Collection and Management of Highway Maintenance Fees'";
13. Detailed Rules for the Implementation of the Collection and Management of Highway Maintenance Fees in Jiangsu Province;
14. Provisions on the Collection and Use of Road Transport Management Fees.

Defendant's Defense: The Meeting Minutes do not fall within the scope of administrative litigation. On August 20, 2002, against the backdrop of a large-scale conflict triggered by the opening of rural public transport and the extension of urban public transport at Nanyang Town Transportation Management Station, Jiangsu Province, coinciding with the Public Transport Company, the Municipal Government convened a special meeting with the responsible heads of relevant departments, at which the Meeting Minutes were produced. The Minutes, based on relevant laws, regulations, documents, and urban planning provisions, defined the scope of urban public transport, clarified the respective responsibilities of the construction, transportation, and other departments concerning urban public transport and road transport management, and proposed a plan for resolving the disputed issues. This act constitutes an internal guiding act of an administrative organ, does not exceed the provisions of relevant laws and regulations, does not constitute a specific administrative decision, and has no administrative coercive force. The plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., lack the standing to sue in this case and are not entitled to raise claims regarding whether the Urban District Transportation Bureau can investigate and punish the Public Transport Company or whether such investigation and punishment are lawful. Their assertion that the Municipal Government unlawfully interfered with the Transportation Bureau's investigation and punishment of the Public Transport Company is also without any factual or legal basis.

The regulatory documents provided by the defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, included:

1. Reply of the Urban Construction Administration Bureau of the Ministry of Urban and Rural Construction and Environmental Protection, Urban Public Transport Document [1984] No. 016, "Reply on the Scope of Urban Public Transport Operations";
2. Several Provisions on Strengthening Urban Public Transport Work, Urban Development Public Document No. 309 [1980];
3. Reply of the Ministry of Construction, Construction City Letter [2001] No. 93, "Reply on Issues Related to Urban Public Transport Operations";
4. Article 59 of the Organic Law of the Local People's Congresses and Local People's Governments of the People's Republic of China;
5. Article 3 of the Regulations of Jiangsu Province on Road Traffic Management;
6. Regulations of Jiangsu Province on the Administration of the Road Transport Market;
7. Measures of the State Council for Handling Official Documents of State Administrative Organs, State Council Document [2000] No. 23.

After the court examined the evidence and conducted cross-examination during the trial, neither party raised any objection to the authenticity of the evidentiary materials and regulatory documents submitted by the other side. The authenticity of the above evidentiary materials was lawfully affirmed. Based on the above evidence, the Yancheng Intermediate People's Court found the following facts:

On August 20, 2002, after a conflict arose between the extension of rural public transport into the city and urban public transport, the defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, convened the relevant departments for coordination and produced the Meeting Minutes. The Meeting Minutes defined the operational scope of urban public transport in Yancheng City and specified that urban public transport operating within the defined planning area would continue to be exempted from relevant transportation charges.

Additionally, it was found that the plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., were road transport operators approved by the transportation department. The passenger transport routes they operated overlapped, in the area from east of the overpass in Yancheng City's urban area to Nanyang Town, Yancheng City's urban area, with the routes of Routes 5 and 15 of the Public Transport Company, which were explicitly exempted from transportation charges by the Municipal Government. On August 20 and 21, 2002, the Urban District Transportation Bureau issued a notice and a letter to the Public Transport Company, respectively, requiring the company's vehicles operating on urban roads to complete relevant operating procedures within a specified time limit. The Public Transport Company replied to the Urban District Transportation Bureau on August 20, 2002, stating that, based on the documents of the Ministry of Construction and the spirit of the Municipal Government's Meeting Minutes, the company did not need to go through relevant approval procedures with the competent transportation authority. On September 10, 2002, Ji Deren et al. submitted an application to the Urban District Transportation Bureau, the Urban District Transport Control and Inspection Brigade, and the Nanyang Central Transportation Management Station, requesting the investigation and punishment, in accordance with the Regulations of Jiangsu Province on the Administration of the Road Transport Market, of the Public Transport Company's actions of extending operations beyond the urban area onto Provincial Highway 331 without the approval of the transportation department. On September 11, 2002, the Urban District Transportation Bureau replied to the application of Ji Deren et al., reporting on the above notices and letters to the Public Transport Company, and explaining in the reply that the Meeting Minutes issued by the Municipal Government on August 30, 2002 had clearly stated that "several routes opened by urban public transport within the planning area shall ensure normal operation and continue to be exempted from relevant transportation charges." Because Nanyang Town had been included within the city's construction planning area, the bureau was unable to manage the acts of urban public transport vehicles entering the Nanyang section of Provincial Highway 331.

The main issues in dispute between the parties in this case were: (1) Whether the Meeting Minutes were an actionable administrative act. (2) Whether the relevant contents of the Meeting Minutes were lawful. (3) Whether the Yancheng Municipal Government had unlawfully prevented the Urban District Transportation Bureau from conducting its investigation and punishment.

The plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., argued that: The Meeting Minutes included two specific administrative acts and one internal act that had been externalized. These acts had a substantive impact on their lawful rights and interests, making them actionable administrative acts. Moreover, because their right to fair competition in road transport on the same section of road had been infringed upon, they had standing in this case. The Meeting Minutes' explicit provision allowing urban public transport that had extended its operations onto highways to continue to be exempted from transportation charges contradicted existing laws, regulations, and the documents of the Ministry of Communications, constituting an unlawful administrative act. The defendant's internal act had already been externalized, objectively preventing the Urban District Transportation Bureau from investigating and punishing the Public Transport Company's illegal acts, thereby infringing upon their right to fair competition.

The defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, argued that: The Meeting Minutes were an internal guiding act of the government, had no specific target of application, and were an abstract administrative act. The Meeting Minutes had no direct impact on the plaintiffs and did not create a legal interest under administrative law. Thus, the act was not subject to litigation. The relevant contents clarified in the Meeting Minutes were consistent with laws, regulations, and documents concerning urban construction, and there was no illegality. There was no fact of preventing the transportation department from investigating and punishing the Public Transport Company; rather, the Urban District Transportation Bureau itself recognized that the Public Transport Company's acts were not illegal and voluntarily ceased its investigation and punishment.

The Yancheng Intermediate People's Court held:

Although the Meeting Minutes of the defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, were formally meeting materials distributed to subordinate governments and various departments, their content defined the scope of urban public transport operations within the municipality and explicitly stated that transportation charges would continue to be exempted within the defined scope. Moreover, this act had actually caused the Urban District Transportation Bureau's management of the Public Transport Company to be suspended. Therefore, the Meeting Minutes constituted an administrative decision, had specific enforcement content, and were an actionable administrative act. The plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., as operators carrying out road transport on the same section of road as the beneficiary of the defendant's administrative act—the Public Transport Company's Routes 5 and 15—filed a lawsuit on the grounds that the Municipal Government's act infringed upon their right to fair competition. Thus, they had standing as plaintiffs in administrative litigation.

The defendant, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, acting in accordance with the provisions of the Organic Law of the Local People's Congresses and Local People's Governments of the People's Republic of China, convened the relevant subordinate departments for coordination to resolve a conflict and produced the Meeting Minutes. The Minutes defined the operational scope of public transport as within the urban planning area and specified that public transport vehicles operating within the above scope would continue to be exempted from charges. This was a clarification and reaffirmation of a pre-existing objective fact and an administrative act performed within the defendant's statutory authority. It did not violate relevant laws or regulations. The plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., lacked a legal basis for seeking a finding that the act was unlawful.

Although the Urban District Transportation Bureau had objectively suspended its investigation and punishment of the Public Transport Company's operations beyond the urban area, the plaintiffs, Ji Deren et al., had failed to submit sufficient evidence in this case to prove that the defendant had unlawfully interfered with the transportation management department's investigatory work. Therefore, their request for a finding that the Municipal Government had unlawfully forced the Urban District Transportation Bureau to suspend its investigation and punishment was without factual basis.

In summary, although Ji Deren et al. had standing to sue in this case, their claims could not be sustained. Pursuant to Item (4) of Article 56 of the Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Implementation of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the court rendered the following judgment on December 22, 2002:

Judgment: The claims of the plaintiffs, Ji Deren, Cai Yuehua, Cai Heping, and Ding Shuquan, are dismissed.

The litigation costs of RMB 100 shall be borne by the plaintiffs, Ji Deren, Cai Yuehua, Cai Heping, and Ding Shuquan.

After the first-instance judgment was announced, Ji Deren et al. appealed to the Jiangsu High People's Court.

Appellant's Allegations: The first-instance judgment's finding that the relevant contents of the Yancheng Municipal Government's Meeting Minutes were not unlawful is clearly erroneous. A series of regulatory documents from the Ministry of Communications sufficiently demonstrate that the operational scope of urban public transport should be "strictly defined within the city's urban area" rather than within the urban planning area. Three regulations from the Ministry of Communications, the Ministry of Finance, and other ministries and commissions all require that when urban public transport vehicles leave the urban area and travel on highways, they must pay highway maintenance fees, passenger transport surcharges, and transport management fees. The Urban District Transportation Bureau was compelled to cease its investigation and punishment of the illegal acts due to the relevant content of the Meeting Minutes. The provision in the Meeting Minutes concerning exemption from charges seriously infringed upon our right to fair competition. The appellants requested a finding that the Yancheng Municipal Government's act of forcing the Urban District Transportation Bureau to suspend its investigation and punishment of the Public Transport Company's unlawful operations was unlawful; and a finding that the relevant content of the Yancheng Municipal Government's Meeting Minutes violated the relevant provisions of the state's road transport management.

Appellee's Defense: According to the relevant provisions of the Ministry of Construction, the territorial scope for the operation of urban public transport vehicles is the urban planning area. The relevant provisions of the Meeting Minutes reflect the principles of proceeding from reality, seeking truth from facts, and taking the overall situation into consideration, and are fully consistent with the law. The appellants lack standing to sue in administrative litigation and are not entitled to raise claims. The Meeting Minutes constitute an internal guiding act of the administrative organ and have no administrative coercive force. The provisions in the Meeting Minutes concerning the division of functions between the construction and transportation departments and the continued exemption from relevant transportation charges are abstract. This case is not within the scope of administrative litigation.

Since neither party objected to the facts found by the first-instance judgment, the Jiangsu High People's Court affirmed those facts.

The issues in dispute between the parties in this case were: (1) Whether the provision in the Meeting Minutes concerning the exemption from charges for public transport vehicles within the planning area was actionable. (2) Whether Ji Deren and the other four persons had standing as plaintiffs. (3) Whether the provision in the Meeting Minutes concerning the exemption from charges for public transport vehicles within the planning area was lawful.

The Jiangsu High People's Court held:

An administrative guiding act refers to an act with the nature of consultation, suggestion, instruction, or admonition performed by an administrative organ in the course of administrative management, which does not have administrative coercive enforcement. In contrast, the provision in the Meeting Minutes of the appellee, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, concerning the exemption from charges for public transport vehicles within the planning area was clearly required to be implemented. Therefore, the Yancheng Municipal People's Government's assertion that this act constituted an administrative guiding act lacks legal basis. This exemption provision was a decision directed at the specific subject of the Public Transport Company and addressed the specific matter of whether routes opened by the Public Transport Company within the planning area were required to pay transportation charges. The above content of the Meeting Minutes had in fact directly granted the Public Transport Company the benefit of exemption from transportation charges within the planning area and should not be deemed an abstract administrative act. Furthermore, since the Meeting Minutes were an act conferring rights upon one party, and the Public Transport Company, as the beneficiary, also participated in the meeting, although the Meeting Minutes were not directly served on the interested party, the relevant content of the Meeting Minutes was subsequently implemented. The Urban District Transportation Bureau had already informed Ji Deren et al. in writing of the reasons why it could not administratively manage the Public Transport Company and of the content of the Meeting Minutes. Therefore, the act of the Yancheng Municipal Government in the Meeting Minutes of granting exemption from fees for public transport vehicles within the planning area should be deemed a specific administrative act subject to litigation.

Since the passenger transport routes of Routes 5 and 15 of the Public Transport Company overlapped with the passenger transport routes operated by Ji Deren et al., competitive relations between the parties in their operations objectively existed. The fact that the Public Transport Company might be operating at a low profit or at a loss does not negate the competitive relationship between the parties. Ji Deren et al., believing that the provision in the Meeting Minutes exempting the Public Transport Company from relevant transportation charges led to unequal competition, filed an administrative action. This is consistent with the provisions of Item (1) of Article 13 of the Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Implementation of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China. As business operators who had obtained operating permits, the economic interests of Ji Deren et al. were closely related to the operating revenue of their vehicles. They were entitled to file an administrative action on the grounds that the administrative act of the Yancheng Municipal People's Government infringed upon their right to fair competition. Whether unfair competition actually existed does not affect their exercise of the right to sue. The first-instance judgment's finding on this point is lawful. The Yancheng Municipal People's Government's argument that Ji Deren et al. lacked standing as plaintiffs cannot be sustained.

The dual management and dual structure phenomena existing in China's current traffic management system are historically formed, i.e., the competent department of construction administration manages urban public transport, while the competent department of communications administration manages road transport. Currently, there is still a problem of segmenting the passenger transport market between departments and between urban and rural areas. Although this management system does not conform to the principle of "unification, simplification, and efficiency," it should be recognized before being unified by a competent authority, and both the construction and communications administrative departments should perform their duties in accordance with the law. Article 8 of the Highway Law of the People's Republic of China provides that the competent department in charge of communications under the State Council shall be in charge of highway work nationwide. The competent department in charge of communications under the local people's governments at or above the county level shall be in charge of highway work within their respective administrative regions. According to the provisions of the State Council on the functions of the Ministry of Communications, the Ministry of Communications is the competent administrative department for the highway transportation industry. Therefore, as the competent administrative department for the local highway transportation industry, the competent departments of communications at all levels have the authority, in accordance with relevant national laws, regulations, and policies, to be responsible for the industry management of highway transportation; to maintain a fair competition order in the highway transportation industry; and to be responsible for highway administration and the collection and management of traffic fees. Consequently, all vehicles operating on highways managed by the communications department must accept the administrative management conducted by the communications department in accordance with the law. With the acceleration of urbanization and the needs of public travel, it has become an objective reality that urban public transport operates beyond the original urban districts. Therefore, the operational scope of urban public transport and the scope managed by the communications department are not necessarily incompatible; it cannot be considered that roads within the operational scope of urban public transport are necessarily excluded from the administrative management of the communications department, nor can it be considered that roads managed by the communications department cannot necessarily be the operational scope of urban public transport. The definition of the operational scope of urban public transport does not affect the management of roads under the jurisdiction of the communications department. When urban public buses leave the urban roads built and maintained by the urban construction department as defined in the Urban Road Management Regulations and operate on roads built and maintained by the communications department, they must also accept the management implemented by the communications department in accordance with the Highway Law and relevant road transport regulations, and go through the corresponding procedures as prescribed. According to Articles 9 and 10 of the Provisions on the Administration of Collection of Highway Maintenance Fees jointly formulated by the Ministry of Communications, the State Planning Commission, the Ministry of Finance, and the State Price Bureau, public buses traveling on urban roads built and maintained by the urban construction department are exempt from highway maintenance fees, but if public buses travel across highways for a distance of within 10 kilometers, the maintenance fee is assessed at one-third of the full amount. The No. 5 and No. 15 buses of the Public Transport General Company leave the urban area and enter Provincial Highway 331, managed by the communications department, traveling nearly 10 kilometers, so they should pay highway maintenance fees in accordance with the law. According to the Notice on Regulating the Surcharges for Highway Passenger and Freight Transport to Increase Highway Construction Funds, jointly formulated by the State Development Planning Commission, the Ministry of Finance, and the Ministry of Communications, public buses are not vehicles exempt from highway passenger and freight transport surcharges and should pay such surcharges. The Reply on Issues Concerning the Scope of Collection of Highway Passenger Transport Surcharges (Document No. 885 [1998] of the General Office of the State Development Planning Commission) and the Urban Construction Department of the Ministry of Construction Answers the Public Utilities Bureau of Chengde City, Reiterating the Definition of Urban Public Transport (Document No. 98 [1996] of the Urban Construction Department of the Ministry of Construction) provided by the Yancheng Municipal Government can only prove that vehicles operating within urban areas may not pay such fees, and do not involve public buses operating outside the urban area. At the same time, according to the relevant provisions of the Provisions on the Collection and Use of Highway Transport Management Fees (Document No. 633 [1986] of the Ministry of Communications and the Ministry of Finance), vehicles operating on highways must also pay transport management fees. Therefore, when urban public buses leave roads built or maintained by the construction department and enter roads managed by the communications department, they should pay highway maintenance fees, passenger and freight transport surcharges, and transport management fees in accordance with the law. The communications department has the statutory authority to collect the above fees and to exempt fees through statutory procedures; no other administrative organ has the authority to collect or exempt such fees, nor does it have the right to decide that an entity obligated to pay regulatory fees should be exempt from such fees.

In summary, the provision in the Meeting Summary of the Yancheng Municipal People's Government that "several lines opened by urban public transport within the planned area shall ensure normal operation and continue to be exempt from relevant traffic regulatory fees," as an administrative decision of the government, has administrative coercive force and is an actionable specific administrative act. Ji Deren and others, as operators whose operational scope overlaps with that of the public transport vehicles owned by the Public Transport General Company, have the right to file an administrative lawsuit on the ground that the provisions of the Meeting Summary infringe upon their right to fair competition. The provision in the Meeting Summary of the Yancheng Municipal People's Government regarding the exemption of regulatory fees within the planned area exceeded statutory authority. The content of this decision lacks a legal or regulatory basis and is inconsistent with the several provisions of the relevant national ministries and commissions mentioned above, and should therefore be revoked in accordance with the law. The first-instance judgment correctly found that Ji Deren and others had standing as plaintiffs and that the relevant content of the Meeting Summary constituted an actionable specific administrative act. However, its determination of the legality of the provision in the Meeting Summary regarding the exemption of regulatory fees within the planned area was incorrect and should be revoked in accordance with the law. The other claims of Ji Deren and others, having no legal interest related to their right to fair competition, are not supported.

Accordingly, in accordance with Article 61, paragraph (2) of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China and Article 56, paragraph (4) of the Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Implementation of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Jiangsu High People's Court rendered the following judgment on May 19, 2003:

1. The administrative judgment of Yancheng Intermediate People's Court, Jiangsu Province (No. 052 (2002) Yan Xing Chu Zi) is revoked;
2. The decision in Article 5 of the 13th Special Meeting Summary of Yancheng Municipal People's Government that "several lines opened by urban public transport within the planned area shall ensure normal operation and continue to be exempt from relevant traffic regulatory fees" is revoked;
3. The other claims of the appellants are dismissed.

The case acceptance fees for the first and second instances total RMB 200, to be borne equally by the four appellants, including Ji Deren, and the appellee, Yancheng Municipal People's Government, each paying RMB 100.
